\section{The Linker's View of the File}

John R. Levine divides object-file contents into headers, code and data, relocations, symbols, and optional information for debugging. His ELF discussion then distinguishes sections, which organize data for linking, from segments, which describe what a loader maps \cite[chapter~3]{levine2000linkers}. A relocatable file is an input to a linker, not a program image ready to enter. For ordinary native ELF objects, start with the section headers rather than trying to infer the layout from program headers. Conversely, a loadable image's segments can cover several sections; names such as \texttt{.text} are not themselves load commands.

Levine illustrates 32-bit ELF. In the ordinary AMD64 ELF64 model here, the processor supplement specifies little-endian encoding, ELF class 64, and machine identifier \verb|EM_X86_64|. Use the header to establish class, byte order, machine, and file type before interpreting the rest. A section header describes, among other things, the section's type, flags, file offset, size, and alignment. A \verb|SHT_NOBITS| section can describe storage without containing that many bytes in the file. Its header's logical size is not a bound for reading a payload. Section names and symbol names are offsets into their respective string tables, not pointers or interchangeable indices.

\section{Follow Indices, Not Naming Conventions}

A regular symbol table records a name offset, value, size, binding, type, visibility, and section index, with some attributes sharing a field. In an \verb|ET_REL| object, an ordinary defined symbol's value is an offset in the section indicated by its index. Undefined and absolute symbols have special indices rather than storage there. A relocation section's \verb|sh_info| names the section to modify and its \verb|sh_link| names the symbol table used by its records. Read those indices instead of deducing the target from a name such as \verb|.rela.data|. Each entry identifies its patch offset within the target section, the relocation type, and a symbol by table index.

Levine's example contrasting implicit and explicit addends describes 32-bit x86 when it mentions \texttt{REL}. The AMD64 ABI in use here specifies \texttt{RELA}, whose addend is in the record. Its type selects both the calculation and field format. For the absolute \verb|R_X86_64_64| type, the calculation is $S+A$ and the field is eight bytes. Do not add the bytes that happen to be in the field to $S+A$. An assembler fixup is an internal deferred operation; only an ABI relocation left in the output is visible in this table.

