\section{Follow a Data Reference Through a Partial Link}

Create \texttt{slot.s} with the following contents:

\begin{verbatim}
.section .data.fixture,"aw",@progbits
    .byte 0x7e
    # Seven bytes of padding belong to this input section.
    .balign 8, 0
.globl entry_slot
entry_slot:
    # Record a reference two bytes into another input's data.
    .quad tone + 2
\end{verbatim}

Create \texttt{tone.s} using the same named section, with an eight-byte contribution:

\begin{verbatim}
.section .data.fixture,"aw",@progbits
.globl tone
tone:
    .ascii "SALT"
    .zero 4
\end{verbatim}

Run these commands with GNU Binutils in a scratch directory:

\begin{verbatim}
as --64 -o slot.o slot.s
as --64 -o tone.o tone.s
readelf -hSWsr slot.o
ld -r -o joined.o tone.o slot.o
readelf -hSWsr joined.o
\end{verbatim}

In \texttt{slot.o}, \texttt{tone} is undefined and \verb|entry_slot| has value eight. The relocation starts at section offset eight, uses \texttt{tone}, and has addend two. In \texttt{joined.o}, \texttt{tone} is defined at offset zero. The eight-byte prefix moves the slot and relocation site to $8+8=16$, while the addend remains two. The partial link can find the definition but cannot substitute an absolute address: the combined section is still movable. If it is eventually placed at address \texttt{0x402000}, this record would put \texttt{0x402002} into the slot, in little-endian bytes \texttt{02 20 40 00 00 00 00 00}. That number is an address into \texttt{tone}, not the section offset of the field storing it.

Trace the reference without trusting the pretty-printed name in the relocation line. In the section dump, follow \texttt{Inf} to the data section and \texttt{Lk} to the symbol table. Split the relocation's ELF64 \verb|r_info| value at bit 32: the high half gives a symbol index and the low half gives the relocation type, which is one here. In one GNU Binutils run the index changed from two to six after the partial link, even though the type and addend stayed the same. Check the high half against the symbol table in \emph{each} object; the observed index values are not portable toolchain guarantees.

\Cref{fig:visual-284} shows which table supplies each part of the reference in \texttt{joined.o}.

