\section{Preserve Relations When Rewriting Tables}

An object reader must check table extents, resolve indices against the appropriate table, and verify that each supported relocation's field fits in its target section. For example, offset sixteen with an eight-byte field fits a section of size twenty-four exactly, but does not fit one of size twenty-three. The check can compare the offset to the size minus the field width after ensuring the width fits, avoiding overflow in computing the end.

Levine's Chapter~3 project calls for a round trip that preserves meaning rather than byte identity, and specifically warns to update relocation indices when reordering symbols. The invariant for a rewritten object is that each relocation still designates the same logical field and the same symbol or permitted equivalent expression after remapping. Neither an unchanged symbol-name list nor an unchanged table of numeric indices tests that invariant on its own.
